(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-09"] = {"7.1":{"s":"The limit of f(x) as x approaches a is the value that f(x) gets closer and closer to when x is near a but not equal to a. We can come from the left side or the right side. The limit exists only if the left-hand limit and the right-hand limit are both there and equal. The limit does not depend on what f(a) is, or even on whether f(a) is defined.","w":["The limit exists only when the left-hand and right-hand limits are equal.","The limit at a point does not need the function to be defined at that point."]},"7.2":{"s":"To find a limit, first try putting x = a. If this gives 0/0, change the form of the expression before substituting again. Common ways are factorising and cancelling the common factor, rationalising a square root by multiplying with its conjugate, and using a standard limit. The limit of a sum, a product or a quotient can be found from the limits of the parts, if the denominator's limit is not zero.","f":[{"x":"lim(x -> a) (x^n - a^n)/(x - a) = n*a^(n - 1)","n":"Standard algebraic limit, a > 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.157"}],"w":["0/0 is not an answer: it only means the expression must be simplified before substituting.","Cancel the common factor (x − a) first; only then put x = a."]},"7.3":{"s":"Limits of trigonometric functions use the key limit of sin x over x as x goes to 0, which is 1. The angle must be in radians. From it follow the limits of tan x over x, of sin kx over x, and of (1 − cos px) over x squared. The limits of sin x and cos x at a point a are simply sin a and cos a, because these functions are continuous.","f":[{"x":"lim(x -> 0) (sin x)/x = 1","n":"Valid only when x is in radian. Also lim(x -> 0) (tan x)/x = 1.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.157"},{"x":"lim(x -> 0) (sin k*x)/x = k","n":"k is a constant. Similarly lim(x -> 0) (tan k*x)/x = k.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.157"},{"x":"lim(x -> 0) (1 - cos p*x)/x^2 = p^2/2","n":"p is a constant.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.157"},{"x":"lim(x -> 0) (cos m*x - cos n*x)/x^2 = (n^2 - m^2)/2","n":"m and n are constants.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.157"}],"w":["The limit sin x / x = 1 holds only for x in radians, never for degrees.","sin kx / x gives k, not 1 and not 1/k."]},"7.4":{"s":"Limits with exponential and logarithmic functions rest on three basic results. The limit of (aˣ − 1) over x as x goes to 0 is log a, and it becomes 1 when a = e. The limit of log(1 + x) over x is 1. The limit of (1 + x) to the power 1/x is the number e. Most problems are reduced to one of these by a substitution or by taking out a constant.","f":[{"x":"lim(x -> 0) (a^x - 1)/x = log a","n":"For a > 0; log means the natural logarithm.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.157"},{"x":"lim(x -> 0) (e^x - 1)/x = 1","n":"The case a = e of the previous limit.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.157"},{"x":"lim(x -> 0) log(1 + x)/x = 1","n":"Natural logarithm.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.157"},{"x":"lim(x -> 0) (1 + x)^(1/x) = e","n":"The number e is about 2.718.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.157"}],"w":["The limit of (aˣ − 1)/x is log a, not a; for (eˣ − 1)/x it is 1.","The limit of (1 + x) raised to 1/x is e only when x goes to 0, not when x goes to ∞."]},"7.6":{"s":"As x grows without bound, 1/x goes to 0. To find the limit of a ratio of polynomials as x tends to infinity, divide the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of x that occurs. Terms with x in the denominator then vanish. The limit is a finite number when both have the same degree, 0 when the denominator has the higher degree, and the limit is infinite when the numerator's degree is higher.","f":[{"x":"lim(x -> ∞) 1/x = 0","n":"The basic limit at infinity; use it after dividing by the highest power of x.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.157"}],"w":["∞ is not a number: never write ∞ − ∞ = 0. Rationalise first.","Compare the degrees of numerator and denominator before doing any work."]}};
